===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*reh₁d-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|ratte}}, {{m|enm|rat}}, {{m|enm|rotte}}, from {{inh|en|ang|rætt}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*ratt}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*rattaz}}, {{m|gem-pro|*rattō}} (compare {{cog|fy|rôt}}, {{cog|nl|rat}}), of uncertain origin, possibly from {{der|en|ine-pro|*reh₁d-|t=to scrape, scratch, gnaw}}. However, the rat may have been unknown in Northern Europe in antiquity, and the Proto-Germanic word may have referred to a different animal; see {{m|gem-pro|*rattaz}} for more.<ref name="Kroonen2011">Guus Kroonen, ''The Proto-Germanic n-stems'' (2011), page 222</ref> Attestation of this family of words begins in the 12th century. {{rfv-etym|en}}

Some of the Germanic cognates show considerable consonant variation, e.g. {{cog|gml|ratte}}, {{m|gml|radde}}; {{cog|gmh|rate}}, {{m|gmh|ratte}}, {{m|gmh|ratze}}.<ref name="Kroonen2011"/> The irregularity may be symptomatic of a late dispersal of the word, although Kroonen accounts for it with a Proto-Germanic stem ''*raþō'' nom., ''*ruttaz'' gen.,<ref name="Kroonen2011"/> showing both [[w:Indo-European ablaut|ablaut]] and a [[w:Kluge's law|Kluge's law]] alternation, with the variation arising from varying remodellings in the descendants. Kroonen states that this requires a {{der|en|ine-pro|-}} etymon in final ''*t'' and is incompatible with the usual derivation from {{der|en|ine-pro|*reh₁d-|t=to scrape, scratch, gnaw}}.<ref name="Kroonen2011"/>

